<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.11">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="8.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.16" />
    <!-- Shrinks drill diagrams on upload. Pinned to 3.x deliberately: 4.x moved to a paid licence
         and fails the build with "No Six Labors license found" until you buy one. 3.1.x is the
         Six Labors Split License — free for individuals and orgs under $1M revenue.
         Must also stay fully managed: Dockerfile.fast publishes on the host and assembles onto a
         linux/amd64 base, so a package with native per-RID assets would build fine and fail at
         runtime. Do not "upgrade to latest" without reading this.

         THE "still gets security fixes" PART OF THIS NOTE WAS WRONG, corrected 3 Oct 2026.
         3.1.12 is the last release of the 3.1 line and carries five open advisories, three of
         them High. Every one is patched only in 4.1.2, so no 3.x fix is coming.
         A vulnerability scan of this project will keep reporting them.

         Four of the five cannot be reached from this app: two are TIFF CCITT encoders and this
         app only ever encodes WebP, one needs Image<HalfVector4> plus HistogramEqualization which
         it never calls, and the ICC one is reached on 3.x through the IccProfile.Entries accessor,
         which it never touches. The fifth, GHSA-wmxv-xphr-5c9g, is a BigTIFF decoder that can be
         made to spin on a 24-byte file — and that one WAS reachable, because ImageSharp's default
         configuration registers nine decoders while this app accepts four.
         PlanStorageService.DecodeFormats now registers only those four, which removes it without
         a licence. See README "What I'd flag" and known-issues #14.

         So: upgrading is a licence decision, not a version bump, and the thing to re-check before
         taking it is whether a NEW advisory lands on JPEG, PNG, GIF or WebP. Those are the four
         still being decoded. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SixLabors.ImageSharp" Version="3.1.12" />
    <!-- Uploads the nightly volume archive to Cloudflare R2 over the S3-compatible API.
         Pinned to an EXACT version, not 3.7.*: the request shape changes inside that range.
         3.7.305 has no RequestChecksumCalculation; 3.7.511 does, which marks AWS's default
         request-checksum rollout and is a known source of failures against non-AWS S3
         endpoints. S3BackupStore sets it to WhenRequired explicitly so the default can never
         move under us on an upgrade. If an upload ever fails with a checksum or signature
         error, that setting is the first thing to look at.
         Fully managed with a net8.0 target and no runtimes/ native assets, which is what the
         ImageSharp note above requires of every package here. -->
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.S3" Version="3.7.511.8" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
